Environment variables: set, read, ignored

A few variables steer debug output, test clocks, progress drawing,
child identity, and radio peers. Each entry below says what the
variable does when you need it, and what goes wrong when you get it
wrong. Exact shapes and edge rules follow the plain meaning. Every
site below was read in full before writing.

When to set what

IMG_DEBUG - extra image detail after a failure. Scope: whole
run, set it yourself per run.

CONCORD_CLOCK_OFFSET - skew one node clock in a test. Scope:
one spawned child only, never production.

NO_COLOR or TERM - plain scrollable output. Scope: your shell.

ROS_DOMAIN_ID - point weld children at a DDS domain. Scope:
forked weld children only.

XDG_CONFIG_HOME - isolate node identity per run. Scope:
forked children or one-shot shell prefix.

IMG_DEBUG

Extra image debug output for post-mortems. Leave it unset for normal
runs, which stay quiet. Set it when an image push or manifest step
failed and you need the response code, body, and headers.
(Concord image registries today; the helper itself is
registry-generic). Any value
enables it, even empty: presence is the switch. If you export it
globally you will drown normal output in debug lines, so set it for
one run only.

Shape: presence check only. Any value, even empty, enables it.
Absent means quiet. Effect when set: failed steps print [img] <step>
failed, and manifest retries print response code plus body plus
headers. Set it to 1 for the whole run when you need
full detail. Scenarios stay quiet by default; probes set
it for post-mortems.

CONCORD_CLOCK_OFFSET

Concord-only test time hook. It skews one Concord node into the
future or past so the harness can prove the system handles
clock drift. The Concord binary reads it; the harness sets it
for the skewed node only. This variable means nothing to other
binaries: weld and ROS peers ignore it. Never set it
in production: a binary on fast or skewed timers is a different
system than production, so timing verdicts from skewed runs do not
transfer. Mechanism tests stay valid on fast clocks; timing verdicts
do not. Mark fast runs logic-only. If you set this by hand on every
node, you have not tested skew, you have only moved the wall clock.

Shape: offset seconds as text, for example "300". The spawn path
sets it in the child before exec, only when the caller passed a non-
null offset. The skew spawn form runs the named skew node with the
offset; all other nodes get true time (null means no setenv). The
call returns false when the skew node is out of range or the offset
is null.

NO_COLOR

Turn off color and cursor codes in the rsuite progress view when you
log output, pipe it, or use a terminal that mangles escapes. Set it
to any value, even empty, to disable. Unset means color when the
terminal allows. If output looks garbled with escape litter, set
this and rerun rather than parsing around the codes.

Shape: any value, even empty, disables. Set means no ANSI colors and
no cursor or erase codes at all; rows scroll as plain text. See TERM
next.

TERM

Terminal type for progress output. You normally never set this by
hand; your terminal already sets it. A value of dumb means the
terminal cannot draw, so rsuite scrolls plain rows like NO_COLOR
does. An unset TERM means not dumb. Any other value means not dumb.
If colors vanish on a smart terminal, check NO_COLOR first: any set
value also disables, even on a smart term.

Shape: exact string dumb means dumb; unset means not dumb; any other
value means not dumb. Effect: dumb disables all escape sequences
plus color, like NO_COLOR. Colors in the CLI summary and failure
lines follow the same rule, so they obey it too.

ROS_DOMAIN_ID

DDS domain for weld echo and pub children so parallel runs and
neighbors do not hear each other. The weld driver sets it in each
forked child before exec; you never export it yourself for runs. The
same value also passes as --domain on the weld command line. If two
runs share a domain on one host, their chatter leaks across runs and
observed checks flap. If peers never meet, check that both sides
share the intended domain.

Shape: plain int text such as "7". The value is built with decimal
formatting. Shell form: ROS peer setup builds ROS_DOMAIN_ID plus
RMW, AMENT, library, and PATH prefixes for sourcing the ROS install
before running peers. Scope: child env only; the parent never
exports it.

XDG_CONFIG_HOME

Per-node config home so real binaries do not share identity.
Each forked child gets its own node directory under the run
statedir, where the driver seeds that system's identity
material (Concord seeds CA certs plus member config; weld
uses its own node home the same way). Without this
isolation, nodes would share keys and state and the test would prove
nothing about a real cluster. Set it per child or as a one-shot
shell prefix, never globally: a global export points every node at
one identity.

Shape: the node dir (net statedir plus node name). Drivers seed
per-system identity under it. Shell form: CLI helpers prefix
commands with XDG_CONFIG_HOME plus the dir, for example the node
rotate-key helper, workload stop, and the suite stop path. Scope:
child env or one-shot shell prefix; the parent keeps its own env.
Some check files set it the same way for legacy spawn paths;
check files are frozen and keep copies.

Negative list: read but not used

These names look meaningful but change nothing. Do not chase them
when debugging. Setting them wastes time and hides the real knob,
which lives in the workflow steps, the Makefile, or a fixed path
below.

TMPDIR is ignored. No getter for it exists in the repo. Paths are
fixed: STATEDIR is /var/tmp/resonance, rsuite logs default to
/var/tmp/resonance-rsuite-XXXXXX via mkdtemp, run lock plus counter
live at /var/tmp/resonance-runs.lock plus per-id locks at
/var/tmp/resonance-run-N.lock, and doctor probes /var/tmp.
Setting TMPDIR changes nothing. Note: /tmp appears only in a header
comment warning that the /tmp tmpfs is nodev and cannot hold device
nodes.

CI is ignored. No getter for it exists in C sources, the Makefile,
or the workflow file. CI behavior comes from the workflow steps, not
from env detection.

/tmp/opencode probe path

Fixed scratch path for the C23 compiler probe, not a setting. Wipe
it any time; the standard target recreates it. If the probe fails,
fix the compiler, not this path.

make standard uses /tmp/opencode for its C23 probe. The recipe runs
mkdir -p /tmp/opencode then pipes a small version-gate program into
the compiler with -std=c23 to build /tmp/opencode/c23_probe. It is a
fixed path, not env driven.

Code map

IMG_DEBUG reads: image fail path and manifest retry loop;
set it per run when debugging. CONCORD_CLOCK_OFFSET set
site: sim spawn path (Concord only); skew spawn form and
header skew notes.
NO_COLOR and TERM reads: progress
no-escape and color helpers. ROS_DOMAIN_ID set sites: weld echo and
pub spawns, plus ROS peer shell prefix. XDG_CONFIG_HOME set sites:
sim, weld, and concord spawn paths, CLI helper prefixes, suite
stop path.
Fixed paths: statedir and run lock plus counter in sim sources,
rsuite log dir in the CLI, probe path in the standard rule.
